# Java sources in Ports/iOSPort/src that the native macOS (AppKit) port does NOT
# compile. Everything not listed here is shared -- see shared-natives.exclude for
# why the list is an exclusion rather than an inclusion.
#
# This list is short on purpose, and shorter than the natives one.
#
# The UIKit-bound Java classes -- Lifecycle, IOSVirtualKeyboard, IOSGLSurface,
# IOSCarBridge, MacWindowManager, CatalystWindowNative -- still ship here, because
# IOSImplementation references them directly and excluding them simply does not
# compile. That is safe rather than sloppy: every native they declare is defined
# in the shared IOSNative.m behind a platform guard, so the symbol exists on a
# macOS build and the body is a no-op. MacImplementation overrides
# getWindowManager() to return the AppKit manager, so the Catalyst one is never
# instantiated and its no-op natives are never reached.
#
# Prising those classes out of IOSImplementation is worth doing -- it is what
# would let the macOS port stop carrying dead UIKit bindings at all -- but it is
# a refactor of the shared implementation, not a manifest entry, and it does not
# block the port from working.

# The port's own implementation factory. Each port ships one, under the same
# fully-qualified name, shadowing the core one so Display instantiates that
# port's implementation. Two of them on one compiler source path is a duplicate
# class; two on one translator classpath would be whichever wins.
com/codename1/impl/ImplementationFactory.java
